## plan_task «the passport»

Plan «the passport» (or all I typed after the command, if a client cut it at a space), with what we have said about it, as one task in Tisty. Ask `rules` «tasks» first. Then `find` whether a task already covers it, the archive included: an open one takes the new steps with `plan` instead of a second task, and a closed one is read for how it went. Otherwise `propose` one task with its steps in order — parts only for pieces finished on their own — in the list it belongs in (`lists`; ask me when none fits), with a date only if I gave one. Then tell me its title, its list and its steps.

## plan_task

Plan the work we have been talking about as one task in Tisty. Ask `rules` «tasks» first. Then `find` whether a task already covers it, the archive included: an open one takes the new steps with `plan` instead of a second task, and a closed one is read for how it went. Otherwise `propose` one task with its steps in order — parts only for pieces finished on their own — in the list it belongs in (`lists`; ask me when none fits), with a date only if I gave one. Then tell me its title, its list and its steps.

## solved_before «the passport»

How did we solve «the passport» (or all I typed after the command, if a client cut it at a space) before? Search Tisty before trying anything: `find` reaches the archive, where every finished task is kept whole, and the documents on the subject. `read` the closest tasks for how they ended, their steps and their notes, and `outline_doc` the documents that cover it. Tell me what was done, when, and where it is written, by title and list or folder. If nothing turns up, say so plainly rather than guessing.

## solved_before

How did we solve the problem we are on before? Search Tisty before trying anything: `find` reaches the archive, where every finished task is kept whole, and the documents on the subject. `read` the closest tasks for how they ended, their steps and their notes, and `outline_doc` the documents that cover it. Tell me what was done, when, and where it is written, by title and list or folder. If nothing turns up, say so plainly rather than guessing.

## keep_decision «the passport»

Keep the decision we just made in Tisty, in the one document about «the passport» (or all I typed after the command, if a client cut it at a space). Ask `rules` «documents» first. `find` that document, `outline_doc` it, and write the decision with `edit_doc` in the passage it belongs to, dated and with its reason; a decision it replaces is struck through and dated, never left standing beside it. If no document covers the subject, ask me which folder it belongs in before `write_doc`. Tell me the document's title and folder.

## keep_decision

Keep the decision we just made in Tisty, in the one document on its subject. Ask `rules` «documents» first. `find` that document, `outline_doc` it, and write the decision with `edit_doc` in the passage it belongs to, dated and with its reason; a decision it replaces is struck through and dated, never left standing beside it. If no document covers the subject, ask me which folder it belongs in before `write_doc`. Tell me the document's title and folder.

## leave_off «the passport»

In Tisty, leave the open task about «the passport» (or all I typed after the command, if a client cut it at a space) — `find` it, and ask me if several fit — where the next session can pick it up. `read` it first. `tick` every step that is finished. `note` what was done, what was tried and ruled out and why, and what comes next, in that order. If every step is done and the work holds, `say_done` with the proof. A decision that outlasts the task goes in its document, changed in place with `edit_doc`, and the note says where. Change nothing else, and tell me in a few lines what you left.

## leave_off

In Tisty, leave the task we have been on where the next session can pick it up. `read` it first. `tick` every step that is finished. `note` what was done, what was tried and ruled out and why, and what comes next, in that order. If every step is done and the work holds, `say_done` with the proof. A decision that outlasts the task goes in its document, changed in place with `edit_doc`, and the note says where. Change nothing else, and tell me in a few lines what you left.

## pick_up «the passport»

Pick up where I left off in Tisty. Call `catch_up` first, with no `since`: it brings what moved since you were last here and who moved it. Then `find` the open task about «the passport» (or all I typed after the command, if a client cut it at a space) — ask me if several fit — and `read` it with its steps, its page and its latest notes. Tell me in a few lines what moved, where the task stands, what was done last and what comes next, naming tasks by title and list, never by id. Change nothing yet.
